WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.680
When creating a video game, an animated movie,&nbsp;
or to create any believable virtual world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:17.880
among many other things, we need geometry.&nbsp;
Tons and tons of geometry. Both to store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.320
and to render images of our&nbsp;
characters and the environment.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.480
And having lots of geometry&nbsp;
means a big headache. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.880
we can use traditional methods that store&nbsp;
all the data needed for these objects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:41.460
and they also render super fast. But, there is a&nbsp;
problem. What is the problem? Well, size! These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:47.400
can take gigabytes that will eat not only your&nbsp;
storage but your cellular data plan as well.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:53.340
Now, do not despair, modern learning-based&nbsp;
methods are coming to the rescue in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:59.820
form of NERFs. That is, Neural Radiance Fields.&nbsp;
Here, we don’t need to store all the geometry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.940
just a few photos, and miraculously&nbsp;
they can fill in all the missing data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:13.380
so … can that really be? Can we fly through&nbsp;
these photos? Yes we can! This is amazing.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:19.920
Now, it gets better, these are instant NERFs.&nbsp;
These can be trained in a matter of minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:27.780
sometimes seconds, and rendered also in&nbsp;
seconds. And, look, we are light transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:34.920
researchers over here, so our keen eyes see&nbsp;
that specular and refractive objects work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:42.540
really well too. That is fantastic, and folks&nbsp;
at Luma AI have already made an app that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:48.960
create these NERFs right on your phone. And&nbsp;
some of these seem either production quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.420
or really close to it. Yes, that’s&nbsp;
right, all this comes from a paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.420 --> 00:02:01.020
that was published just two years ago. This&nbsp;
is incredible progress in just two years.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:06.480
But wait. So, these NERF representations are&nbsp;
much smaller than traditional techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.300
however, they still put some strain on&nbsp;
your cellular data plan. And, don’t forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.080
they require considerable&nbsp;
horsepower to render quickly.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.660
But, good news, neural networks are&nbsp;
excellent at compression. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:28.380
NVIDIA already has a neural network that can&nbsp;
help us compress even ourselves! You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:34.860
in this earlier work, the technique takes only&nbsp;
the first image from our video, and it throws away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:41.940
the entire video afterwards! But before that,&nbsp;
it stores a tiny bit of information from it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:48.780
which is, data on how our head is moving&nbsp;
over time, and how our expressions change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:55.500
That is an absolutely outrageous idea,&nbsp;
except the fact that it works. With this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.020
our video data for a video call is thus&nbsp;
at least as detailed as the traditional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:07.080
state of the art techniques, but uses&nbsp;
a third as much data, or even less!

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:14.640
And, get this in this new paper, scientists at&nbsp;
NVIDIA claim to solve both of our NERFs problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:22.440
at the same time! Compact and fast NERFs at&nbsp;
the same time! Now I will believe that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:28.380
I see it. Can they put the incredible compression&nbsp;
capabilities of neural networks to more use here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:37.980
Let’s see. Whoa, look at that! You see a previous&nbsp;
NERF-based method, and here, the new method. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:44.220
can that really be? Am I seeing correctly&nbsp;
or am I dreaming? This new method requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:51.120
60 times less data to show us this geometry.&nbsp;
And the quality, as described by the signal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:04:00.120
noise ratio is nearly the same. Not the same,&nbsp;
but very close. And 60x cheaper. My goodness.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.760
And it gets even better. That was just&nbsp;
the compression part. What about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.460
fast rendering part? Well, these models&nbsp;
are so small, we can start downloading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:17.280
multiple versions of them at the same time,&nbsp;
and the coarsest version can be shown to us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:24.660
after receiving only the first 10 kilobytes&nbsp;
of data. That is equivalent to downloading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:32.340
less than one second of music. Wow. And then,&nbsp;
as more data arrives, the finer details start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:39.600
to appear over time. And we are through the&nbsp;
whole process very, very quickly. Incredible.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:46.140
But wait, we are experienced Fellow Scholars over&nbsp;
here, so we know that there are other papers on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:52.740
this topic. For instance, what about Plenoxels?&nbsp;
How does it compare to that? This is Plenoxels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:58.200
which is an amazing paper that we talked&nbsp;
about earlier in this series. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.940
way, interestingly, this earlier method did not&nbsp;
use neural networks to achieve what you see here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:10.860
which is incredible. And now, look at that! This&nbsp;
new one, look, it can go at least as far with 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:19.140
megabytes of data as Plenoxels can go with over a&nbsp;
150. And Plenoxels is not just some ancient paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:25.680
it is from just one year ago. Seeing this&nbsp;
kind of improvement just one year and one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.740
more paper down the line, that is the&nbsp;
power of human ingenuity. I love it.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:38.640
And you see, this might be the future of imagery.&nbsp;
We don’t store the whole geometry of the objects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:46.560
anymore, we just take a few photos, and let the AI&nbsp;
fill in the rest. Bravo! What a time to be alive!

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

